On 10.04.2012 01:13, Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> 
> It's not a particularly difficult task, but it's one that you have
> to complete in order to finish the game.  Also, IMO, Vlad's tower
> is not boring like Gehennom.  (The fact that it's small probably
> helps with that.)

The fact that it's small makes it possible to wait for a cockatrice
corpse and stone the whole inhabitants in one walk, thus getting all
carried items from the vampires for free.

> 
> I mean clearing all the levels down to there -- exploring them
> and killing all the monsters on them.  Yes, I know speed runners
> probably don't bother with this, but that omission would very
> much complicate the ascension run for them.

I am far from being a speed runnner (40-60k turns IIRC) but exploring
and clearing all Gehennom would make me certainly judge like all folks
who think Gehennom is boring. My suggestion is; just don't do it.

> 
>> Gehennom is short if you don't explore it completely.
> 
> Gehennom is not short.  Gehennom is at least twenty levels long,
> and furthermore each of those twenty levels takes several times
> as long to explore as a typical pre-Castle level.
> 
> If you're *skipping* most of it every single time, in order to
> avoid being bored to tears, and arguing that this is what everyone
> should do, then you're making my point for me.  You wouldn't be
> skipping as much of it as you can possibly manage to skip if it
> weren't boring.

Sorry, I really cannot see an argument for you here. What I propose is
that you should not thoroughly explore it; skipping is something else.

If you just want to say that the number of levels is too large for the
time you usually spend there, I also cannot agree for (at least) two
reasons; first, in case you are missing items you can spend more time
to find them[*], and the Mysterious Force would be irrelevant as well,
then[***].

[*] I've done so a few times. Once, I recall, when I have blown up my
bag of holding by accident and needed to get some more equipment for
the ascension. Another time when I needed a source of magic resistance
that I didn't found throughout the upper dungeons[**]. And a third time
when I needed some more candles than available.

[**] Though I died by a touch of death.

[***] The force may of course be considered a Bad Thing as well, from
some folks. *shrug*

> A really successful revision of Gehennom, IMO, would be one that
> results in people no longer *wanting* to skip large parts of it.

I can agree with that.

>> Usually you can dig short-cuts all the way...
> 
> The demon-prince lairs actively prevent this by having gratuitous
> undiggable walls blocking the places you'd most like to dig. [...]

But that is only an issue with Asmodeus' lair. (And to escape that you
can use the cursed potion of gain level as suggested.)

> The maze levels allow digging, but you have to do it the slow way [...]

I think that's fine.

>> You don't need to thoroughly explore them; there are a couple ways
>> to make that process a lot easier.
> 
> Why even *have* such areas in the game if the player is not
> expected to explore them?

It's part of the freedom you have that Nethack offers. If you are a
newbie you can look into every corner and collect everything to make
you confident to be prepared for the ascension. In case you are an
experienced player you can do as much as necessary. I really don't
think that this degree of freedom is bad, rather the opposite is the
case; I think that this is an interesting [probably even constituting]
part of the game.

>> Potions for alchemy I consider to be very interesting.
> 
> Yes, but at that point in the game they're mostly interesting in
> the aggregate, not individually [...].

(Yes, you will have to collect them.)

> [...]
> Right, I wasn't thinking of changing 15+ maze levels into 15+
> room-and-corridor levels.  I ws thinking more of mixing them
> in here and there.

That is one of the main features that Nethack is lacking; more types
of random layouts.

In the past there was also the suggestion of a sewer type layout, which
would be nice somewhere between the Castle and Gehennom. (I had written
a sewer generator once, but in C++, not in C to be easily incorporated
into the code base.) Is there any sewer implementation around meanwhile?

> [...]
> I don't want to *skip* Gehennom.  Or, rather, maybe in its current
> state I kind of do, but ultimately I don't want to have an area in
> the game that everyone just wants to skip.  I'd rather make the
> area interesting, so that people aren't inclined to skip it.

Ah, okay. (Substitute "skip" by "not thoroughly explore" and that still
makes sense.)

> Currently, you don't need to economize with wands of digging,
> because once you reach Gehennom you have (almost) no further use
> for them until the Plane of Earth. 

(On the Plane of Earth you need not more than two fully charged wands of
digging, IME.)

> In the main dungeon, a wand
> of digging is much *faster* to use than a pick-axe.  In Gehennom,
> it's not, unless you're concerned about the turn count (e.g.,
> for speed-running stats). [...]

Not quite. Switching forth and back between pick-axe and melee weapon is
not always a good option, and the turn-count is usually not a relevant
factor but on the ascension run you don't want any delays; using wands in
such circumstances is often a sensible move.

> [...mazes of width two...]
> Algorithmically, it's straightforward:  I could very easily write
> a Perl script that spits out ASCII maps for such levels.  But the
> level generation code in NetHack appears upon cursory inspection
> to be rather hairy, and modifying it is probably painfully hard.

Indeed. That's why I've done my sewers generation standalone and postponed
the integration into the code base until the never-day.

Janis


